cription: 'E007 open_questions: 1 is arguable — create-wizard test coverage depends\n34\t    on the unresolved E001 field-set outcome and E002 reuse/rebuild outcome; rationale\n35\t    claims feature behavior is fully specified.'\n36\terror: null\n37\t---\n38\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-8007\n39\t\n40\t## Review Summary\n41\t\n42\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n43\tRecommendation: revise\n44\t\n45\tTriage consistency: `triage` is null (full decomposition) with `epic_count: 8` — no\n46\ttriage contradiction. Contract validator: OK\n47\t(`python3 skills/epic-decompose/scripts/validate_decomposition.py RHAISTRAT-8007`\n48\texits 0 — body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, and\n49\tpriority inheritance all pass).\n50\t\n51\tThe decomposition is strong on coverage, boundaries, ACs, and completeness. It fails\n52\ton one gating-metadata consistency defect (Criterion 4): the same Investigation\n53\t(E002, prototype alignment) is classified as a non-gating scheduling dependency for\n54\tE005 but a true gate for E006, with no material distinction between the two consumer\n55\trelationships. One of the two classifications must be wrong.\n56\t\n57\t## Criterion Details\n58\t\n59\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n60\tAll 8 HLRs trace to epics: HLR-1 → E005; HLR-2/3/4/5 → E006; HLR-6 (P1) → E005/E006\n61\tACs plus E007 enforcement; HLR-7 (P1) → E005/E006/E007; HLR-8 (P2) → E005. The\n62\ttraceability matrix is present and matches epic HLR Traceability sections. HLR-8 (P2)\n63\tis bundled into the P0 wizard epic with explicit justification (same form surface —\n64\tsplitting would produce two epics building the same form); this is incidental polish\n65\ton the P0 work, not a deferrable independent feature, so no priority collapse.\n66\tHLR-6/HLR-7 are cross-cutting P1s mapped as ACs on each affected surface — correct\n67\tshape. Priority inheritance holds: E001/E002/E003 (all P0) block P0 work; E004 (P0)\n68\tblocks E007 (P0) and E008 (docs-authoring, exempt, P1 from strategy priority Major —\n69\tcorrect mapping). No issues.\n70\t\n71\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n72\tDiagram/frontmatter cross-check: every edge in the mermaid diagram\n73\t(`graph TD`, dependency → dependent) has a matching entry in the target epic's\n74\t`dependencies`, and vice versa — all 13 edges verified bidirectionally, no\n75\tfrontmatter dependency references a nonexistent epic, no branch files exist. No\n76\tcircular dependencies. Critical path length 4 (E001→E005→E007→E008) is within the\n77\tM-standard heuristic (3-4). Parallelism is well handled: E004 (RBAC/feature-flag\n78\tmanifests) is correctly NOT chained to E005/E006 — coupling is enforced via the\n79\t\"end-to-end CRUD verified against a cluster with the extended ClusterRole applied\"\n80\tAC, matching the config-reference rule; E005 ↔ E006 are parallel per same-repo/\n81\tdifferent-surface; E001 → E006 omission is justified (detail view renders the full\n82\tspec, no curation decision). Rule 11 edges to E008 are present from all five\n83\tmain-plan Implementation epics. No konflux/license/repo-onboarding epics exist, so\n84\tthe mandated-parallelism pairings (rules 7/8/10) do not apply. One minor\n85\tobservation: E001's field-set outcome plausibly shapes E003's wizard wireframes, but\n86\tE003 has no relationship to E001; the decomposition treats the field set as a design\n87\tvariable (stated in E003's ai-signals rationale), which is defensible — see issues\n88\tlist.\n89\t\n90\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n91\tEach epic is a clean component/team tuple: E001 (Feature Store / FS Eng),\n92\tE002+E004+E005+E006 (AI Core Dashboard / Dashboard Eng), E003 (UXD / UXD Team),\n93\tE007 (QE / QE Team), E008 (Documentation / Docs Team) — all canonical component\n94\tnames. No epic bundles multiple teams. E006 groups list/detail/delete/status, but\n95\tthese are one management surface in one repo for one team — same logical change,\n96\tsprint-sized. E005 (wizard) and E006 (management pages) are separate surfaces, not\n97\tduplicated scope. No work-product type mixing: E003 is pure design assets, E008 is\n98\tpure docs authoring, code epics contain no content authoring. No oversized epics\n99\t(M strategy, 3-5 sprints across 8 epics). No issues.\n100\t\n101\t### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\n102\tE001 and E002 are genuine Investigations whose outcomes change downstream work\n103\t(field set defines E005's scope; prototype alignment determines reuse vs rebuild).\n104\t`gated_by` targets are in direct `dependencies` and `gate_failure_impact` has both\n105\t`action` and `fallback_approach` populated where set. However, there is a major\n106\tinconsistency in how E002 is classified across its two consumers:\n107\t\n108\t- For **E005**, the decomposition explicitly demotes E002 to a non-gating scheduling\n109\t  dependency: \"Prototype alignment determines how much of draft PR #7013 can be\n110\t  reused vs rebuilt — a sequencing/efficiency input, not a scope gate... if the\n111\t  prototype diverges from the formal approach, the wizard is built directly on the\n112\t  formal approach with unchanged scope and acceptance criteria.\"\n113\t- For **E006**, the same finding is declared a true gate (`gated_by: E002`, action\n114\t  `rewrite`), with fallback \"rebuild the affected pages from the dashboard's\n115\t  established K8s list/detail/watch patterns instead of carrying prototype code\" —\n116\t  which is precisely the same reuse-vs-rebuild efficiency concern ruled non-gating\n117\t  for E005.\n118\t\n119\tThe two consumer relationships are materially identical (both E005 and E006 say\n120\t\"reuses the validated engineering prototype wherever the alignment assessment\n121\tconfirms fit\"; E006's scope and ACs are likewise unchanged by a divergent prototype —\n122\tthe pages would be built directly on the formal approach). Therefore one of the two\n123\tclassifications is wrong: either E005 is missing `gated_by` coverage for the E002\n124\tInvestigation dependency, or E006's `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` is a scheduling\n125\tdependency misfiled as a gate. Per the review rules this is a major issue — an\n126\tInvestigation dependency without `gated_by` on a downstream epic is major, and a\n127\tspurious gate is a scheduling dependency that belongs in `dependencies` only — so\n128\tthis criterion scores 0. Correction: pick one reading (the E005 rationale argues\n129\tboth should be non-gating scheduling dependencies) and align E005/E006 accordingly.\n130\t\n131\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n132\tType-appropriate signal fields are present and correct throughout: E001/E002 carry\n133\t`investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric); E003-E008 carry `ai_signals` (9-signal\n134\trubric). All eight ai-signals files exist and their rationale tables match the\n135\tfrontmatter values. Spot checks hold up: E001 `source_accessibility: 1` (public CRD\n136\tschema and operator source) and `human_judgment_required: -1` (PM sign-off on the\n137\tfield subset) are justified; E002 `cluster_hardware_dependence: 0` (code-reading\n138\tassessment) is consistent with its scope; E005/E006 `open_questions: -1` match their\n139\tgated status; E008 `human_process_gates: -1` matches the docs editorial process.\n140\tTwo arguable-but-defensible values noted as minors: E003 `open_questions: 0` (the\n141\tfield-set decision shapes wizard form layout; the \"field set as a design variable\"\n142\trationale is stated but thin) and E007 `open_questions: 1` (create-wizard test\n143\tcoverage depends on E001's unresolved field-set outcome). Neither contradicts the\n144\trubric. Arithmetic/thresholds not checked (computed by pipeline).\n145\t\n146\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n147\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy's proposed acceptance\n148\tcriteria. Rule-mandated ACs: the docs-authoring epic (E008) has both technical\n149\treview by engineering and editorial review ACs; no replacement epics exist (new UI\n150\tsurface — feature-flag ACs are present anyway on E005/E006); no konflux chain\n151\t(build-pipeline AC N/A). The canonical soft-AC trap is handled correctly: the UXD\n152\ttimeline external-schedule constraint sits in E003's Risks/Notes and the summary's\n153\tHealth Warnings, not as an AC. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID\n154\t(epics refer to \"the CRD form-scope investigation\" etc.; \"PR #7013\" is a GitHub PR,\n155\tnot a draft ID). No issues.\n156\t\n157\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n158\tFull scope coverage: create wizard (E005), list/detail/delete/status (E006), RBAC\n159\textension and feature flag (E004), viewer tier (ACs on E005/E006, enforced and\n160\tbypass-tested in E007), air-gapped support (E005/E006 ACs plus dedicated E007 test\n161\tpass plus E008 instructions), UXD design (E003), QE (E007), admin-guide docs (E008 —\n162\tDocumentation Support: Yes, so Rule 11 docs-authoring epic is present). The\n163\tfeast-operator validation work is folded into E001 with an explicit justification in\n164\tHealth Warnings rather than silently dropped. All six strategy open questions are\n165\taccounted for (field subset → E001; prototype alignment → E002; edit capability →\n166\tresolved per Out-of-Scope with mutability documented informationally in E001;\n167\tbackend restrictions → E001; UXD timeline and performance targets → Health Warnings\n168\twith strategy-grounded answers). Strategy risks are carried forward (UXD timeline →\n169\tE003 risks; CRD stability → E001; air-gapped validation completeness → E005 AC;\n170\tpod resource growth → E007 scope; viewer RBAC bypass → E007 scope). Out-of-scope\n171\titems are correctly excluded. No conditional branches required (no bounded-uncertainty\n172\toutcomes — E001/E002 gates use gate_failure_impact, not branch files). No issues.\n173\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6da3b0b539286d2","agent_id":"ac2adcea6f526cc79","session_id":"5b25fd68-a792-4553-b4a0-957d31cb4ef6","uuid":"0110d157-2cb7-