*E008:** fine.\n\nSo Criterion 5: signals are mostly justified with detailed rationales; 2-3 values are borderline arguable (E001 human_judgment_required -1 vs -2; E003 open_questions 0; E006 open_questions +1). Per the rubric, \"1: Most signals are correct but 1-2 have arguable values\". I count ~3 arguable. Hmm, that might push toward... 3 arguable is more than \"1-2\". But none contradict the rubric outright (score 0 requires contradiction or missing files). Let me pick the strongest: E001's human_judgment_required=-1 calling the field-set decision \"peripheral\" when it's the gate E005 hangs on — that's the most substantive inconsistency. E003 open_questions=0 — defensible via the design-variable argument. E006 open_questions=+1 — defensible. So really 1 solid arguable + 2 weak. I'd give Criterion 5 a 2 or 1. Given rationales are unusually thorough and consistent, and the arguable ones have documented reasoning rather than contradictions, I lean 2... but the E001 one is genuinely contradictory with the gating structure. The rubric for -2: \"the gating question is itself a human/governance decision\". E001's gating output (field-set decision) requires PM sign-off — recorded in its own AC (\"recorded with PM sign-off\"). So the gating question IS a human decision → -2 per rubric, and -1 is scored. That's a rubric contradiction, not just arguable → score 0? Hmm, \"Signal values contradict the rubric conditions\". \n\nWait, let me be careful. E001 has multiple questions. The \"gating question\" — which one gates downstream? The field-set recommendation (curated vs full) gates E005's scope. It needs PM sign-off. So the gating question requires human judgment → human_judgment_required = -2 per rubric. Scored -1. Is the field-set decision \"itself a human/governance decision\"? The investigation produces a technical recommendation; the PM ratifies. One could say the AI can resolve the technical substance and the PM sign-off is the ratification — the decision isn't pure