gating\n32\t    output for E005 (gated_by=E001, action=rewrite). Per the rubric, -2 applies when\n33\t    the gating question is itself a human/product decision; the value is arguable\n34\t    between -1 and -2.\n35\terror: null\n36\t---\n37\t## Review Summary\n38\t\n39\tScore: 11/14 — fail\n40\tRecommendation: revise\n41\t\n42\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py RHAISTRAT-8007` exited 0 — body\n43\tsections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, and priority\n44\tinheritance all pass).\n45\t\n46\tTriage consistency: `triage` is null (full decomposition) with `epic_count: 8` —\n47\tno triage contradiction; multi-epic output is expected.\n48\t\n49\tThe decomposition is thorough and mostly sound: HLR coverage is complete, the DAG\n50\tis internally consistent (frontmatter matches the diagram edge-for-edge),\n51\tparallelism is correctly preserved (notably the E004 config-vs-code decoupling via\n52\tAC instead of a DAG edge), and strategy-level risks/open questions are carried\n53\tforward. It fails on one structural point: the two edges from the prototype\n54\tInvestigation (E002) into the frontend Implementation epics are true Rule 3 gates\n55\t(the outcome changes the implementation approach — reuse vs rebuild) but carry no\n56\tgating metadata, which the automated pipeline cannot see.\n57\t\n58\t## Criterion Details\n59\t\n60\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n61\tAll 8 HLRs map to at least one epic; the traceability matrix matches each epic's\n62\tHLR Traceability section. P0 HLR-1 → E005; HLR-2/3/4/5 → E006 (one management\n63\tsurface). P1 HLR-6 (viewer browse-only) and HLR-7 (air-gapped) are cross-cutting\n64\tand mapped as ACs on each affected surface epic (E005, E006) plus dedicated QE\n65\tvalidation (E007) — appropriate for requirements that are thin increments on the\n66\tP0 surfaces, not independently deferrable features. P2 HLR-8 (inline validation)\n67\tis bundled into E005 with justification — it is incidental polish on the same form\n68\tsurface, so no priority collapse. Priority inheritance is correct: every epic\n69\tblocking P0 work (E001, E002, E003) is P0; E008 (docs-authoring, P1) is exempt and\n70\tcorrectly derives from the strategy's Major priority.\n71\t\n72\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n73\tAll 13 diagram edges match the target epics' frontmatter `dependencies` exactly,\n74\tin both directions; arrow direction follows the dependency → dependent convention.\n75\tNo cycles. Critical path length 4 (E001/E002/E003 → E005 → E007 → E008), within\n76\tthe M-standard 3-4 heuristic. Parallelism is correctly preserved: no E004 → E005/\n77\tE006 edge (RBAC/flag manifests are configuration the frontend does not build\n78\tagainst — coupling enforced by the \"verified against a cluster with the extended\n79\tClusterRole applied\" AC, mirroring the Rule 10/13 pattern); E005 ∥ E006 (Rule 15,\n80\tsame repo, different surfaces); E001 ∥ E003 with the form-scope field set handled\n81\tas an explicit design variable. Rule 11 docs-last is complete: E008 is blocked by\n82\tall five Implementation epics (E003-E007); the two Investigations correctly do not\n83\tgate docs. No conditional branch files. Rule 19 (safety-critical strategy →\n84\tfail-mode + security Investigations) was considered and judged inapplicable: this\n85\tis an admin UI that delegates enforcement to the existing K8s RBAC mechanism; the\n86\tviewer-bypass fail-mode is covered by E004's viewer-tier AC, E007's token-level\n87\tbypass testing, and the strategy-level risk entry. The missing gating metadata on\n88\tthe E002 edges is a Type Correctness defect and is scored under Criterion 4.\n89\t\n90\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n91\tEach component/team tuple produces a separate epic (Feature Store/Investigation,\n92\tAI Core Dashboard ×3, UXD, QE, Documentation). The three same-team dashboard epics\n93\tare split along architectural axes per Rule 2 (kustomize/RBAC manifests vs create\n94\twizard vs management pages) with no scope duplication — the wizard and the\n95\tlist/detail/delete/status pages are distinct UI surfaces and neither rebuilds the\n96\tother's work. E003 (design assets) and E008 (docs) are separated from code\n97\timplementation by work-product type and team; E008's screenshots are incidental to\n98\tthe docs change, not type mixing. No epic appears to exceed a single sprint.\n99\t\n100\t### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\n101\tEpic types themselves are correct: E001 and E002 resolve strategy-flagged\n102\t\"needs validation\" unknowns whose outcomes shape downstream work; E003-E008\n103\tproduce concrete artifacts; E005's `gated_by` (E001) is in its `dependencies` and\n104\tits `gate_failure_impact` has both action and fallback populated. However, the\n105\tgating contract is violated on the E002 edges:\n106\t\n107\t- **E006 lists Investigation E002 in `dependencies` with `gated_by: null`.** The\n108\t  decomposition defends this as a \"non-gating scheduling dependency,\" but Rule 3\n109\t  explicitly classes *approach* changes (not only scope/AC changes) as true gates\n110\t  (`rewrite` if scope/approach changes) — and E006's own description states that\n111\t  on a divergent prototype the pages \"are built directly on the dashboard's\n112\t  established formal patterns,\" i.e., the implementation approach changes\n113\t  (reuse prototype code vs rebuild). An Investigation dependency without\n114\t  `gated_by` is a major issue: automated gate detection cannot see it.\n115\t- **E005 depends on two Investigations (E001, E002) but records gating metadata\n116\t  for only E001.** The E002 dependency is an unrecorded gate for the same reason\n117\t  (a divergent prototype changes the wizard's implementation approach);\n118\t  `gate_failure_impact` covers only the CRD form-scope outcome.\n119\t\n120\tThe fix is straightforward: either record the E002 gate (`gated_by` where null,\n121\tplus `gate_failure_impact` with action=rewrite and the already-written fallback —\n122\tbuild on the formal patterns — for the epic where `gated_by` is already taken by\n123\tthe stronger gate), or restructure so a single recorded gate covers both\n124\toutcomes. The scheduling-only defense cannot stand alongside Rule 3's wording.\n125\t\n126\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n127\tType-appropriate signal sets are used throughout (`investigation_signals` on\n128\tE001/E002, `ai_signals` on all Implementation epics); all eight ai-signals files\n129\tare present with detailed, strategy-grounded rationales; E005's\n130\t`open_questions: -1` correctly reflects its gate. Two values are arguable:\n131\t- E006 `open_questions: +1` (\"all design decisions resolved\") sits uneasily\n132\t  beside its dependency on Investigation E002, whose reuse-vs-rebuild outcome\n133\t  changes the implementation approach — the rubric's -1 condition — and is\n134\t  inconsistent with E005's -1 for the analogous gating investigation.\n135\t- E001 `human_judgment_required: -1` labels the curated-vs-full field-set\n136\t  decision \"peripheral,\" while the DAG treats that same decision as the gating\n137\t  output for E005 (`gated_by: E001`, action=rewrite). Per the rubric, -2 applies\n138\t  when the gating question is itself a human/product decision; the value is\n139\t  arguable between -1 and -2.\n140\t\n141\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n142\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated ACs are\n143\tpresent where applicable: feature-flag visibility ACs on E005 and E006, technical\n144\treview AC on the docs-authoring epic E008 (Rule 24); no konflux chain exists\n145\t(Rule 25 N/A) and no existing capability is replaced (Rule 23 N/A). The UXD\n146\ttimeline — an external schedule constraint — is correctly placed in E003's\n147\tRisks/Notes and the summary's Health Warnings, not misfiled as an AC. No epic\n148\tbody references sibling epics by draft ID.\n149\t\n150\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n151\tAll strategy scope is covered: RBAC/feature-flag